# Hit-card text from Trace decks (Drive cards.jsonl, 31 Oct), with cardfix applied (OBS-24 watch, 25, 35, 37, 39)
C = {}
C['OBS-03'] = ("**Cardiovascular changes in pregnancy:**",
"PARAMETER | CHANGE\n**Cardiac output** | **Up 40–50%**\n**Stroke volume** | Up 30%\n**Heart rate** | Up 15–25%\n**Intravascular volume** | **Up 45%**\n**Systemic vascular resistance** | **Down 20%**\n**Systolic BP** | Minimal change\n**Diastolic BP** | Down 20% at mid-pregnancy; back to baseline at term\n**CVP** | Unchanged\n**Oxygen consumption** | **Up 30–40%**",
"Stroke volume rises first; heart rate keeps rising to term. A \"normal\" blood pressure of 120/80 in the second trimester is relatively high.")
C['OBS-06'] = ("**Supine hypotension syndrome:**\na. Mechanism\nb. Effect\nc. Fix\nd. From when",
"a. The gravid uterus compresses the **inferior vena cava** (less venous return) and the **aorta** (more afterload).\nb. Blood pressure and cardiac output fall; **uteroplacental perfusion** is compromised.\nc. **Left uterine displacement**: a wedge under the **right hip**, or manual displacement of the uterus to the left.\nd. From about 20 weeks (uterus at the umbilicus).",
"In cardiac arrest use MANUAL displacement: a tilt makes compressions less effective.")
C['OBS-07'] = ("**Blood, kidney and gut in pregnancy:**\na. Blood\nb. Kidney\nc. Gut\nd. The risk this creates\ne. Nutrition",
"a. Plasma volume rises more than red cell mass: **dilutional anaemia**.\nb. GFR and renal plasma flow rise by about half; **creatinine falls** (to about 0.4–0.8 mg/dL).\nc. Lower oesophageal sphincter relaxes (progesterone); slow transit; constipation.\nd. **Aspiration**: full abdomen, lax sphincter, slow transit.\ne. A high-calorie state; needs rise in labour and are highest in **lactation**.",
"A creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL is abnormal in pregnancy. Pregnancy is also hypercoagulable: fibrinogen and factors rise.")
C['OBS-08'] = ("**Why is the pregnant airway difficult, and why does she desaturate fast?**\na. The main reason intubation is harder\nb. How much harder\nc. Why desaturation is rapid\nd. Other factors\ne. Practical consequences",
"a. **Oedema, mucosal engorgement, capillary friability**: bleeding and a narrow view.\nb. **2.5 to 3 times** more difficult than in the non-pregnant patient.\nc. Low functional residual capacity (small reservoir) plus high oxygen consumption (fast drain).\nd. Aspiration risk; large breasts; weight gain.\ne. Smaller tube (6.5–7.0), short-handled laryngoscope, ramped position, full pre-oxygenation.",
"GT-3 Q47 keyed the oedema and friability option.")
C['OBS-11'] = ("**Obstetric failed intubation:**",
"**1.** Rapid sequence induction; face-mask ventilation allowed, pressure **under 20 cmH2O**.\n**2.** At most **2 intubation attempts**; a third only by an experienced colleague.\n**3.** Declare failed intubation; call for help; **oxygenate** with a supraglottic airway (at most 2 attempts) or face mask.\n**4.** If oxygenation succeeds: decide whether it is essential and safe to **proceed with surgery, or wake**.\n**5.** If it fails: declare **can't intubate, can't oxygenate**; 100% oxygen; ensure neuromuscular block; **front-of-neck access**.",
"Three algorithms in one line: safe general anaesthesia, failed intubation, can't intubate can't oxygenate. Oxygenation, not intubation, is the goal.")
C['OBS-12'] = ("**Ventilating the pregnant patient:**\na. Plateau pressure\nb. What to avoid\nc. Position\nd. Refractory hypoxaemia\ne. The obstetric option",
"a. Under **30 cmH2O**.\nb. **Permissive hypercapnia**: maternal CO2 retention causes fetal acidosis.\nc. **Prone**: avoids compression by the gravid uterus and of the umbilical vessels, improving fetal blood flow.\nd. **VV-ECMO**.\ne. Expedited delivery may improve the mother's outcome: a multidisciplinary decision.",
"Targets: PaO2 above 70 mmHg (SpO2 95% or more) and PaCO2 30–32. Fetal oxygen delivery depends on maternal oxygen content and uterine flow.")
C['OBS-13'] = ("**Circulation, and the decision to deliver:**\na. Access\nb. Resuscitation\nc. Steroids for fetal lung maturity\nd. Fetal monitoring\ne. Delivery decision",
"a. Two large-bore peripheral cannulae; **avoid the femoral route**; left uterine displacement.\nb. Crystalloids, vasopressors and inotropes **as in any patient**.\nc. **Two doses of betamethasone, 24 hours apart**.\nd. Biophysical profile.\ne. Multidisciplinary; correct coagulopathy first.",
"The drug is betamethasone (12 mg IM, two doses), not beclomethasone. Femoral route avoided because the uterus compresses the vena cava: lines go above the diaphragm.")
C['OBS-15'] = ("**Define pre-eclampsia.**\na Blood pressure\nb Timing\nc Proteinuria\nd Without proteinuria\ne Eclampsia",
"a Systolic **140 or more** or diastolic **90 or more**, on **two occasions 4 hours apart**.\nb After **20 weeks**, in a previously normotensive woman.\nc **300 mg or more in 24 hours**.\nd New hypertension with any one of: platelets under **100,000**; creatinine over **1.1** or doubled; transaminases **doubled**; pulmonary oedema; cerebral or visual symptoms.\ne Pre-eclampsia plus **seizures**.",
"Proteinuria is no longer required. Up to 8% of pregnancies worldwide; 5–15% in Indian hospital practice; mostly healthy primigravidae.")
C['OBS-16'] = ("**The hypertensive disorders of pregnancy:**",
"DISORDER | DEFINING FEATURE\n**Chronic hypertension** | Present **before pregnancy or before 20 weeks**, or persisting more than **12 weeks** after delivery\n**Gestational hypertension** | After 20 weeks, no proteinuria or organ dysfunction\n**Pre-eclampsia** | After 20 weeks, with proteinuria or organ dysfunction\n**Superimposed pre-eclampsia** | Chronic hypertension that acquires proteinuria or severe features\n**Eclampsia** | Seizures",
"Severe features: BP 160/110 or more, platelets under 100,000, creatinine over 1.1, doubled transaminases, pulmonary oedema, cerebral or visual symptoms.")
C['OBS-17'] = ("**Pathophysiology of pre-eclampsia:**",
"**1. Impaired trophoblast invasion**: the spiral arteries stay narrow.\n**2. Placental ischaemia**.\n**3.** The placenta releases **sFlt-1 and soluble endoglin**: anti-angiogenic.\n**4.** Systemic **endothelial dysfunction**.\n**5.** Kidney: **glomerular endotheliosis** (proteinuria). Liver: microcirculatory failure. Brain: oedema, headache, stroke. Vessels: vasoconstriction, high SVR.",
"Risk factors: diabetes, chronic hypertension, BMI over 30, existing cardiovascular disease. Prevention: calcium, low-dose aspirin.")
C['OBS-18'] = ("**Antihypertensives for severe hypertension in pregnancy:**",
"DRUG | DOSE | ONSET | CAUTION\n**Labetalol** | 10–20 mg IV, then 20–80 mg every 10–30 min (maximum **300 mg**); or 1–2 mg/min infusion | **1–2 min** | **Asthma, bradycardia**, heart failure\n**Hydralazine** | 5 mg IV or IM, then 5–10 mg every 20–40 min (maximum 20 mg); or 0.5–10 mg/h | 10–20 min | Maternal **hypotension**, headache, abnormal fetal heart tracing\n**Nifedipine** | 10–20 mg orally, repeat in 20 min; then every 2–6 h (maximum 180 mg/day) | 5–10 min | Reflex **tachycardia**, headache",
"GT-2 Q66 and GT-4 Q51 both keyed IV labetalol. \"No asthma, no bradycardia, no conduction disease\" in a stem is the licence for labetalol. ACE inhibitors and ARBs are contraindicated; atenolol is avoided; nitroprusside is a last resort.")
C['OBS-19'] = ("**Managing pre-eclampsia: the principles.**\na Definitive treatment\nb The fluid dilemma\nc Fluid rule\nd Monitoring\ne Magnesium in pre-eclampsia without severe features",
"a **Delivery**.\nb Volume-depleted despite oedema: **avoid diuretics**; but aggressive fluid causes pulmonary oedema.\nc Isotonic fluid to replace urine output and insensible loss; no more than **80 mL/hour**.\nd Strict input–output charting: prone to renal dysfunction.\ne **Not needed** unless severe features develop.",
"Treat blood pressure to prevent stroke; magnesium to prevent seizures; they are separate jobs.")
C['OBS-20'] = ("**Severe pre-eclampsia: when to deliver?**",
"SITUATION | ACTION\n**34 weeks or more** | **Deliver**\n**Under 24 weeks** | Deliver (not viable)\n**Eclampsia, pulmonary oedema, DIC, renal failure, abruption, non-reassuring fetal testing** | **Deliver immediately**, at any gestation\n**33+0 to 33+6 weeks; HELLP; growth restriction under 5th centile; reversed end-diastolic flow; persistent symptoms; preterm labour or rupture of membranes** | Steroids; **deliver after 48 hours**\n**24 to 32+6 weeks, none of the above** | Steroids; **expectant**; deliver at **33+6 weeks**",
"All get magnesium, antihypertensives as needed, and continuous fetal monitoring.")
C['OBS-21'] = ("**Severe pre-eclampsia: deliver within 72 hours, or manage expectantly?**",
" | DELIVER | EXPECTANT\n**Blood pressure** | **Uncontrolled** severe hypertension; eclampsia | **Controlled**\n**Liver** | AST or ALT over twice normal **with epigastric or right upper quadrant pain** | Over twice normal **without pain**\n**Kidney** | Compromised renal function | Oliguria (under 0.5 mL/kg/h) that **resolves with fluid**\n**Other** | Platelets under 100,000; pulmonary oedema; abruption; persistent headache or visual change | Proteinuria of any amount\n**Fetus** | Repeated late or severe variable decelerations; biophysical profile **4 or less** twice; weight under 5th centile; reversed diastolic flow | Reassuring heart rate; profile 6 or more; weight above 5th centile",
"The AMOUNT of proteinuria never decides delivery. Perinatal mortality 5–11.8%; maternal death up to 1.8%.")
C['OBS-22'] = ("**Eclampsia: the facts.**\na Timing\nb Cases with no preceding pre-eclampsia\nc The seizure\nd The fetus during the seizure\ne The rule",
"a Antepartum **53%**; intrapartum 19%; postpartum **28%**.\nb **20%**.\nc Generalised tonic-clonic, lasting **60–90 seconds**; then confusion or agitation.\nd **Bradycardia** from hypoxia; it usually recovers.\ne A seizure in pregnancy is **eclampsia until proven otherwise**.",
"Proposed causes: intracranial vasospasm, hypertension and ischaemia, vasogenic and cytotoxic oedema, endothelial dysfunction.")
C['OBS-24'] = ("**Magnesium level and effect:**",
"LEVEL (MG/DL) | EFFECT\n**5–9** | Therapeutic\n**Over 9** | **Patellar reflex lost**\n**Over 12** | **Respiratory paralysis**\n**Over 30** | **Cardiac arrest**\nAntidote: **calcium gluconate**. ECG: long PR, wide QRS, long QT, then heart block.",
"Units trap: your electrolyte chapter (MGP-07) is in mmol/L (reflexes above 4, arrest above 13); this table is mg/dL. 1 mmol/L = 2 mEq/L = 2.4 mg/dL, so the two tables roughly agree. In both, the knee jerk goes first.")
C['OBS-25'] = ("**How magnesium works, and the MAGPIE trial:**\na Vascular\nb Neuromuscular\nc Neuronal\nd MAGPIE: design\ne MAGPIE: result",
"a Cerebral **vasodilatation**: relieves vasospasm.\nb Reduces **acetylcholine release** at the motor end-plate.\nc Antagonises intracellular calcium; blocks **NMDA** receptors.\nd About **10,000 women in 33 countries**; magnesium vs placebo in pre-eclampsia.\ne A **58% lower risk of eclampsia** (relative risk **0.42**, 95% CI 0.29–0.60).",
"Because it reduces acetylcholine release, magnesium potentiates neuromuscular blockers. It is the anticonvulsant of choice, NOT an antihypertensive.")
C['OBS-26'] = ("**A seizure in pregnancy: what else?**",
"- **PRES** (posterior reversible encephalopathy).\n- **Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis**.\n- Intracranial haemorrhage.\n- Meningitis, encephalitis; septic encephalopathy.\n- Brain tumour; gestational trophoblastic disease.\n- Drug intoxication or withdrawal.",
"Management of the seizure itself: protect the airway, prevent injury, magnesium, avoid polypharmacy, then follow the delivery plan.")
C['OBS-27'] = ("**HELLP syndrome: criteria.**",
"LETTER | CRITERION\n**H**aemolysis | Abnormal smear (schistocytes); bilirubin **1.2 mg/dL or more**; low haptoglobin; **LDH at least twice** normal\n**E**levated **L**iver enzymes | AST **over 70 U/L** (twice normal)\n**L**ow **P**latelets | **Under 100,000**",
"Occurs in 20% of pre-eclamptics and 0.1–0.2% of pregnancies. AST reflects both liver necrosis and red-cell breakdown. Treatment: early delivery; dexamethasone for fetal lungs; plasma exchange is only a category III indication.")
C['OBS-28'] = ("**Acute fatty liver of pregnancy:**\na Incidence\nb Setting\nc Pathology\nd Symptoms\ne Laboratory\nf Imaging",
"a 1 in **7,000–20,000** deliveries.\nb **Third trimester**; multiple gestation; underweight women.\nc **Microvesicular** fatty infiltration of hepatocytes.\nd Nausea, vomiting, epigastric pain, anorexia, jaundice.\ne Transaminases raised (up to about 1,000); high bilirubin, **ammonia and INR**; **hypoglycaemia**.\nf Bright liver on ultrasound; biopsy is definitive but avoided (bleeding).",
"Treatment: immediate delivery. It can recur. Linked to fetal LCHAD deficiency.")
C['OBS-29'] = ("**HELLP vs acute fatty liver of pregnancy:**",
" | HELLP | FATTY LIVER\n**Hypertension** | **Usual** | In about half\n**Platelets** | **Low, early** | Normal until DIC\n**Glucose** | Normal | **Low**\n**INR, ammonia** | Normal (until late) | **Raised**: true liver failure\n**Haemolysis** | **Yes** | No\n**Encephalopathy** | No | **Yes**",
"Hypoglycaemia, high ammonia and coagulopathy out of proportion to the platelet count point to fatty liver.")
C['OBS-30'] = ("**Antepartum haemorrhage: abruption vs praevia vs rupture.**",
" | ABRUPTION | PLACENTA PRAEVIA | UTERINE RUPTURE\n**Pain** | **Severe, constant** | **Painless** | Sudden; then contractions stop\n**Bleeding** | Dark; **may be concealed** | Bright red, visible | Variable\n**Uterus** | **Tender, firm (woody)** | Soft | Fetal parts palpable\n**Clue** | Hypertension, trauma; **low fibrinogen** | Previous section | Previous scar; in labour\n**Ultrasound** | **Often normal** | Diagnostic | ",
"GT-2 Q26 (your miss): no vaginal bleeding and a negative ultrasound do NOT exclude abruption. Tender firm uterus, shock out of proportion and fibrinogen of 130 make the diagnosis.")
C['OBS-31'] = ("**Postpartum haemorrhage: definition and the four Ts.**",
"Blood loss over **500 mL**; **massive** over 1,000–1,500 mL.\nT | FINDING | TREATMENT\n**Tone** | Soft, boggy uterus | **Massage, uterotonics**\n**Trauma** | Tear, haematoma, inversion | Suture; drain haematoma over 3 cm; replace the uterus\n**Tissue** | Retained placenta | Manual removal, curettage\n**Thrombin** | Blood not clotting | Plasma, platelets, factors; recombinant factor VIIa",
"Atony is the commonest cause. Primary is within 24 hours, secondary from 24 hours to 6 weeks.")
C['OBS-32'] = ("**Uterotonics: dose and the patient to avoid.**",
"DRUG | DOSE | AVOID IN\n**Oxytocin** (first) | 20 IU per litre saline; up to 500 mL over 10 min | Rapid bolus: hypotension\n**Methylergonovine** | 0.2 mg IM | **Hypertension, pre-eclampsia**\n**Carboprost** (PGF2-alpha) | 0.25 mg IM | **Asthma**\n**Misoprostol** (PGE1) | 1,000 micrograms rectally | Few; slower",
"GT-4 Q47: atony after oxytocin, \"no asthma\" in the stem: carboprost. If she were asthmatic the answer shifts; if hypertensive, no ergot.")
C['OBS-33'] = ("**Postpartum haemorrhage: the ladder.**",
"**1.** Prevent: **active management of the third stage** (oxytocin at delivery, controlled cord traction, uterine massage).\n**2.** Resuscitate: two large cannulae, oxygen, monitoring, bloods and cross-match.\n**3.** **Bimanual massage and oxytocin**; find which T.\n**4.** Second uterotonic: carboprost, methylergonovine or misoprostol.\n**5.** Massive: transfuse red cells, platelets and factors; vasopressors.\n**6.** **Tamponade or packing**; compression (B-Lynch) sutures; vessel ligation or **embolisation**.\n**7.** **Hysterectomy**.",
"Tranexamic acid 1 g IV within 3 hours of birth reduces death from bleeding (WOMAN trial). Uterotonics come before balloon tamponade.")
C['OBS-35'] = ("**Amniotic fluid embolism: the facts.**\na Incidence\nb Mortality\nc The classic sequence\nd When\ne Diagnosis",
"a Rare: about **2–6 per 100,000** deliveries.\nb Mortality about **20%** in current registries (range 11–43%); older series reported up to 85%, half in the first hour.\nc Sudden **hypoxia** (respiratory failure), **hypotension or arrest**, **seizures**, then **DIC**.\nd During labour, delivery or within 30 minutes after.\ne Clinical, by exclusion.",
"GT-2 Q23: collapse in labour, seizure, then bleeding from IV sites = amniotic fluid embolism.")
C['OBS-36'] = ("**Amniotic fluid embolism: the mechanism the exam wants.**",
" | OLD VIEW | CURRENT VIEW (EXAM ANSWER)\n**Trigger** | Amniotic debris (lanugo, vernix) through a torn uterine vein | Fetal material entering the maternal circulation\n**Mechanism** | **Mechanical** obstruction of the pulmonary vessels | **Maternal systemic inflammatory (anaphylactoid) response**\n**Phase 1** | | Pulmonary vasospasm, acute right heart failure, hypoxia\n**Phase 2** | | Left ventricular failure, then **DIC** and haemorrhage",
"GT-4 Q33 (your miss): the keyed answer was systemic inflammatory activation by fetal material. NOT mechanical embolisation, NOT IgE-mediated, and tissue factor explains only the DIC.")
C['OBS-37'] = ("**Treating amniotic fluid embolism; sepsis specific to pregnancy.**\na Support\nb Obstetric\nc Rescue\nd Pregnancy-specific sources of sepsis",
"a Aggressive multi-organ support; correct coagulopathy; **massive transfusion protocol**.\nb **Emergency delivery**; caesarean with ligation of uterine or internal iliac arteries, even hysterectomy.\nc **VA-ECMO** as rescue for refractory collapse (case series only).\nd **Chorioamnionitis** (altered vaginal pH, amniocentesis); **pyelonephritis** (ureteric tone lost to progesterone; Gram-negative colonisation); **endometritis** (instrumentation); pneumonia.",
"If she arrests: resuscitative delivery (old name: perimortem caesarean), completed by 5 minutes.")
C['OBS-39'] = ("**Cardiac arrest in pregnancy: what is different?**\na Relieve aortocaval compression\nb Access\nc Airway\nd Magnesium\ne Delivery\nf Fetus\ng What is NOT different",
"a Continuous **manual left uterine displacement**.\nb IV access **above the diaphragm**.\nc Expect a difficult airway: most experienced operator; 100% oxygen; waveform capnography; then **1 breath every 6 seconds** with continuous compressions.\nd If on magnesium: **stop it and give calcium**.\ne **Resuscitative delivery** (old name: perimortem caesarean): if no return of circulation, aim to deliver by **5 minutes**.\nf Detach fetal monitors; call the neonatal team.\ng Compressions, defibrillation energy and drug doses are **unchanged**.",
"Causes A–H: Anaesthetic, Bleeding, Cardiovascular, Drugs, Embolic, Fever, General (Hs and Ts), Hypertension.")

SWEEP = {}
SWEEP['OBS-42'] = ("**Reading the planted cue in an obstetric stem:**",
"CUE IN THE STEM | IT IS TELLING YOU\n**\"No history of asthma\"** | **Carboprost** (or labetalol) is allowed\n**\"No bradycardia or conduction disease\"** | **Labetalol**\n**\"After magnesium, BP remains 176/114\"** | Now treat the **blood pressure**: labetalol\n**\"No vaginal bleeding; ultrasound negative\" with a tender uterus** | **Concealed abruption**\n**Collapse, then seizure, then bleeding from IV sites, in labour** | **Amniotic fluid embolism**\n**\"Despite adequate pre-oxygenation\"** | **Low FRC, high oxygen consumption**\n**Low fibrinogen** | Abruption or embolism with **DIC**\n**Hypoglycaemia with jaundice in the third trimester** | **Fatty liver**, not HELLP",
"Also asked once each: labour epidural infusion uses 0.0625–0.1% bupivacaine (GT-2); cystitis in pregnancy with penicillin allergy is treated with cephalexin (GT-1).")
SWEEP['OBS-43'] = ("**True or false?**\na Functional residual capacity rises in pregnancy.\nb Normal PaCO2 in pregnancy is 40 with bicarbonate 24.\nc The obstetric qSOFA uses a respiratory rate of 25.\nd Magnesium is the antihypertensive of choice.\ne The first sign of magnesium toxicity is respiratory arrest.\nf Amniotic fluid embolism is an IgE-mediated reaction.\ng Carboprost is avoided in hypertension.\nh Ultrasound can be normal in abruption.\ni Resuscitative delivery is done after 15 minutes of CPR.\nj Peripartum cardiomyopathy needs an ejection fraction under 30%.",
"T/F | REASON\na F | It falls by 20%.\nb F | About 30; 18–22.\nc T | \nd F | Anticonvulsant only.\ne F | Patellar reflex.\nf F | Inflammatory, anaphylactoid.\ng F | Carboprost: asthma. Ergot: hypertension.\nh T | \ni F | By 5 minutes.\nj F | Under 45%.", "")
